Revision Notes:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Version 3.0  (23 Nov 94)
-----------

Unbelievable!  The old AC-Basic compiler still chugs away nicely, even
under WB2.0!  And what's more, I can still remember how to use it.

There were several VERY SMALL changes to the program - it no longer
references the s:Metro.config file, a change needed because of the
changing file format to a keyword-type file for the OzMetro program. The
only thing the old door was using from this was the sysop name, so
you'll excuse us if we now replace that with "The Sysop".

The delay after the modem has been opened has been increased - this was
causing problems sometimes (I THINK), and the CHR$(7) printed to the
screen is gone, to avoid possible conflicts with any sound daemons you
may have lurking around (which WAS causing problems under MaxsBeep, if
you use that).

I also have compiled this linking it with the bas.rl file, so it's
standalone.  This file (the ACBasic runtime library) is 40k long, and to
save on executable space, the old door didn't link with it.  You had to
have the file in your l:  directory.  That was okay, because a LOT of my
old doors used it, and you only really needed one copy that all the AC
doors could use.  I thought it easier to link with it for this release
to save you having another setup step.  This is why the exe is HUGE,
BTW!

Note that the highest baud rate you can open COM1: under AC-Basic is
19,200 baud - this door will fail if you're using a higher locked rate
than 19,200 - so if this is the case - don't install it!


Version 2.0  (17 April 1991)
-----------

Oh well!  We did get to another revision.  This has GOT!  to be it, for
sure.  [Hahahahaha....]

I changed a few of the output colours, tidied up the high scores table,
got it to convert the dates to MY format with the month in words, to
avoid all confusion over here (month first - indeed!).

If you make it into the high scores table, it now prints up the high
scores table straight away.  It still leaves behind an icon for the
scores - looked at it and thought, 'what the hell!' Sorry!  Feel free to
delete it, but it'll come back next time the high scores file is written
to disk.

Oh, yeah, it's got local ANSI colour now, so you CAN select ANSI on
local logins and not get the codes printed, rather the colours do change!


Version 1.0  (7 March 1991)
-----------

I imagine this will be the last update for this door. It was the second
door I ever wrote, and a great learning experience. I look back on it
now (only three months later) and can see potential, but it's essentially
a finished product.

There were several major changes.

I put screen clearing codes all through it to make it look cleaner. 

ANSI was added for the remote users, and it looks GREAT calling remote.
Unfortunately, local, the main play lines extend off the far right-hand
side of the screen now. Don't worry, it's only asking you to enter
your move (a number from 1-5 will do). As usual, select NO ANSI when
trying it local.

I added one line to test for 'Q'! Initially, once you had started a 
game, there was no exit until either you got killed, caught the horse
or 99 moves were up. Now you just press 'Q' when it's asking you for
a move and that round will end. Be careful because Q is right next to
the '1'! (Although I tend to use the keypad playing this one).

Changed most of the defaults for Y/n to YES. Not sure why I had it
round the other way.

Added my standard error-file creation routine should the program ever
crash. If it does, look for a file called Door.Errors! in the directory
and it will report error number, time, date and the last line executed.
The line number is NOT really very helpful, but the error number 
certainly is. Error 53=File Not Found. There are only two files this
program uses, so if you get that error, it'll be easy to sort out.

Had a long hard think about it, and decided that since everyone ignores
it anyway, I'd add a shareware plea for the door. Actually, this one
does look good (give it a call remote and see!). It's certainly worth
$5. I have received one shareware donation so far... but I'm gettting
very discouraged with shareware contributions from the USA. You should
look after your Metro door-writers, there aren't many of us!


Version 0.6 (19 December 1990)
-----------

The program had the old carrier detect routines. New ones have been
added.

If the program had someone who caught the horse in less than 10 moves
it would sort the high scores table alphabetically (thus a score of 6
would be put in AFTER a score of 59). (Woops! didn't find this out
for ages).

It was a bit too hard to catch the horse. It's been made a LOT easier.


Instructions:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Corral is a very simple, but addictive game where you are the cowboy
(please excuse the sexism), and you have to catch your horse.

You are represented by a 'C' and the horse by a 'H'. You can move
toward your horse from between 1 & 5 steps at a time, but beware!
Harry (the horse) is quite temperamental. If you move up on him too
quickly (more than half the separation) he will BOLT. He will also
tend to bolt if you pin him up against the rails.

Furthermore, at times, he will lash out and KICK you, thus rendering
you unconscious for a few moves, while he cavorts away, gleefully.+cr$
You CAN win the game, and if you do, you'll be added to a high score
table, which you can call up from the game menu (how do you think I
tested it?)! It's pretty hard, but by no means impossible.


Setup:
~~~~~~

To set this door up on Metro, you must firstly create a directory called
DOORx (where 'x' is the number of the door) in your DOORFILES1 (/2/3/4/5)
directory, and rename the file Door61 to DOORx.

[Under OzMetro, door.exe will do you nicely, just make sure the
directory name of the door is right]

Also required are two files called corral.scores and corral.swine. These
will be altered by the program as things progress, and I'd say you'll get
icons for them, too, but that won't be too much trouble! These, must
naturally be placed in the DOORxx directory in DOORFILES1

Anyway, I'm sure you know how to do all that.

[If in doubt, copy ALL the files from this archive to the DOORxx
directory and you can't go wrong!  Mind you, it only needs
corral.scores, corral.swine and Door.exe.  In fact, thanks to WB2.0, the
corral.swine file isn't actually needed (it is under WB1.3 or less)]

You must have the corral.scores and corral.swine in corral's directory
or the program will abort if it needs to access these files.


Observations:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The program was an old one I used to play on my TRS-80 back in the early
'80's and after I had a look at it I thought it'd lend itself to a Metro
door quite well. With the addition of a high score table so BBS users
could all compete it wouldn't turn out so bad at all. Anyway, I'm glad I
persisted, 'cos it's certainly a worthy inclusion on your doors menu -
unlike 'Shrink' was!

There is also a "Crash the Program" option in Corral. This is just a silly
little routine I put in to learn how to handle BASIC serial files.
After I got it running I thought I may as well leave it in. Just be aware
that the file it creates is going to get larger all the time, so
eventually time will come when you need to delete a big hunk off the top
of it. It should be fairly easy to do with any text editor. It will
take some time before it risks filling the hard drive, 'though! (The file
is 'corral.swine' for obvious reasons when you try it out).

You can keep up with the users who want to crash the system, however,
by reading this section! Keep an eye on them :-)

I must give credit to Percy for all the modem routines, and Mark Turner,
whose high score sort routine I copied just about verbatim from the
Unscramble game. Knew your source would come in handy one day, eh, Mark!
Thanks for that, because that was the one feature that had me stumped.
Only three or four lines, but what a help!

[Well, I must really give Craig Jackson credit for releasing the Wall
Door source so I could pinch his ANSI routines from it.  Craig Jackson
did so much for Metro all those years ago - not least of which was the
Paragon door interpreter!]

Actually, it's amazing how much of the original TRS-80 code is reproduced
in this door. So many years apart, these machines, but not all that
different when it comes to BASIC. (Although the thought of Porting
AmigaBASIC code to the TRS80 doesn't sound like a picnic!)

About the game itself: it gets you in, actually, as it's very hard
to catch the horse, but not so much that it's impossible. I must say it
was difficult to test the high scores routine. Had to sit down and actually
play the thing to get into the high score table to see if it would work!

Anyway, another well-needed metro door! Hey, they're not all that hard to
write! Anyone else want to have a go??? It only took me two days to get
two doors written, and learn about AmigaBASIC which I haven't really
touched much up until now. Never actually 'Finished' a program before!

[Struth, have I come a long way in 3-1/2 years :-)]

[BTW, the window this door opens, opens up two or three pixels down from
the top of its screen - as a result, you can just get to the
screen-to-back gadget, if your mouse pointer is positioned hard top
right]

